WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.936
Security architecture for Go services is
the set of engineering decisions that

2
00:00:04.016 --> 00:00:07.606
shapes how a service protects data,
actions, dependencies, and runtime behavior.

3
00:00:07.686 --> 00:00:09.635
It is not only a diagram or a review
checklist.

4
00:00:09.715 --> 00:00:12.484
It is the way a team decides where trust
begins, where trust ends,

5
00:00:12.564 --> 00:00:13.846
which controls are required,

6
00:00:13.926 --> 00:00:16.952
and how those controls remain
understandable as the service changes.

7
00:00:17.032 --> 00:00:18.878
Go gives service teams useful advantages.

8
00:00:18.958 --> 00:00:22.087
The language is small, the standard
library has strong server patterns,

9
00:00:22.167 --> 00:00:23.962
binaries are straightforward to deploy,

10
00:00:24.042 --> 00:00:27.221
and concurrency can be expressed clearly
when the design is disciplined.

11
00:00:27.301 --> 00:00:30.635
Those strengths help, but they do not
automatically create secure outcomes.

12
00:00:30.715 --> 00:00:33.946
A Go service still needs deliberate
choices around identity, validation,

13
00:00:34.026 --> 00:00:37.308
data exposure, dependency management,
failure behavior, and operations.

14
00:00:37.388 --> 00:00:39.439
A secure service should have clear
boundaries.

15
00:00:39.519 --> 00:00:42.801
Engineers should be able to identify who
can call it, what inputs it accepts,

16
00:00:42.881 --> 00:00:45.241
what outputs it returns, what dependencies
it trusts,

17
00:00:45.321 --> 00:00:47.526
and what assumptions must hold for each
operation.

18
00:00:47.606 --> 00:00:49.401
Inputs and outputs should be controlled,

19
00:00:49.481 --> 00:00:51.737
sensitive data should have visible
handling rules,

20
00:00:51.817 --> 00:00:55.305
and security-relevant behavior should be
observable in logs, metrics, traces,

21
00:00:55.385 --> 00:00:56.154
and audit events.

22
00:00:56.234 --> 00:00:58.901
Architecture also prevents fragile
security by convention.

23
00:00:58.981 --> 00:01:01.135
If authorization checks are scattered
randomly,

24
00:01:01.215 --> 00:01:03.163
if validation happens only in some
handlers,

25
00:01:03.243 --> 00:01:05.500
or if secret handling depends on
individual memory,

26
00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:07.529
the service becomes hard to maintain
safely.

27
00:01:07.609 --> 00:01:10.173
Architecture should make secure behavior
the normal path,

28
00:01:10.253 --> 00:01:13.022
not a heroic effort by the most
experienced engineer on the team.

29
00:01:13.102 --> 00:01:16.282
The goal is a Go service that is
understandable, maintainable, testable,

30
00:01:16.362 --> 00:01:18.516
and safe to operate under real-world
conditions.

31
00:01:18.596 --> 00:01:21.878
Good architecture supports feature work,
incident response, code review,

32
00:01:21.958 --> 00:01:23.753
onboarding, and production operations.

33
00:01:23.833 --> 00:01:27.269
It makes important assumptions explicit so
teams can reason about risk before a

34
00:01:27.349 --> 00:01:28.580
failure, not only after one.
